text:
OG2 · Missing data and the Gini index
In progress. The project analyses the impact of missing data on the Gini index under the three non-response mechanisms (MCAR, MAR and MNAR), and compares complete-case analysis, single imputation and multiple imputation. The missing data simulator illustrates the patterns that motivate this objective:

deleting the cases with missing data (complete-case analysis) is only safe when data are missing completely at random (MCAR);
mean imputation always underestimates inequality, because all imputed values are equal;
deterministic regression imputation also underestimates inequality, because it ignores the residual variability;
stochastic regression imputation and multiple imputation remove most of the bias when the data are missing at random (MAR) and the imputation model includes the variables related to non-response;
when non-response depends on income itself (MNAR), no standard method removes the bias.

Working papers. The problem of missing data in strategic management research: critical issues, understanding and methodological insights (Vendrell, Muñoz, Gomes and Lehman); How to avoid common mistakes in water studies with missing data? (Muñoz, Álvarez, Tortajada and Sarwar).

OG3 · The R package giniVarCI
Achieved. The package giniVarCI is available on CRAN. It:

estimates the Gini index for samples from finite and infinite populations with all the expressions of the literature;
computes variances and confidence intervals with different methods (jackknife, linearisation, bootstrap, empirical likelihood);
computes the Gini index of continuous probability distributions (Beta, Burr, Dagum, F, Fisk, Gamma, lognormal, Pareto, etc.);
draws samples from continuous distributions with a Gini index set by the user;
uses Rcpp, so calculations are faster than in other packages that compute the Gini index.

GiniUs studies how to measure inequality reliably with the Gini index: which estimator to use, how large its bias can be, how to obtain margins of error that can be trusted, and how to deal with missing incomes. The project, funded by the Spanish State Research Agency (PID2022-136235NB-I00), is now in its fourth and last year.
The new website brings together the results of the project and several tools that run in your browser, without installing anything:

a Gini index calculator for your own data, with jackknife and bootstrap confidence intervals and the Lorenz curve;
an interactive bias explorer that reproduces the Monte Carlo studies of the project;
a missing data simulator that compares complete-case analysis, single imputation and multiple imputation under the MCAR, MAR and MNAR mechanisms;
R exercises, including the R package giniVarCI running in the browser;
an inequality observatory with official Gini indices from Eurostat, the World Bank and INE.

In this blog we will publish short posts on new articles, software releases, seminars and practical advice on measuring inequality. You can follow it through the RSS feed or by subscribing.





